{
    "question": "Does LuaEntity.copy_settings carry a locomotive's schedule between two locomotives, and what shape does the copied schedule take in a blueprint?",
    "issue": 115,
    "probe": "tools/oracle/probe-copy-settings-schedule.mjs",
    "apiProbe": "tools/oracle/probe-schedule-api.mjs",
    "binary": "Version: 2.1.12 (build 87038, mac-arm64, steam)",
    "versionCaveat": "Captured on 2.1.12. This editor targets 2.0.45 to 2.0.73 and the corpus declares nothing outside that range. The 2.0 schedule shape (records/interrupts/group) is assumed stable across it and was NOT measured on a 2.0.x binary.",
    "findings": [
        "copy_settings between two locomotives DOES carry the schedule - records, interrupts and the schedule group all arrive on the target.",
        "It REPLACES rather than merges: a target holding its own record ('Gamma') ends up with exactly the source's two records and nothing of its own.",
        "A source with NO schedule CLEARS the target's. The target went from one record to zero and the resulting blueprint has no `schedules` key at all. This is the case an implementation that only ever writes will get wrong.",
        "In the blueprint, two locomotives holding the same schedule are written as ONE `schedules` entry with both entity numbers in `locomotives`, not as two entries.",
        "The 2.0 blueprint shape is an OBJECT - {records, group, interrupts} - not the 1.1 flat array of {station, wait_conditions}. `ISchedule` in packages/editor/src/types.ts still describes only the 1.1 form.",
        "LuaTrain.schedule, the 1.1 property, is nil in 2.1. The API is LuaTrain.get_schedule() returning a LuaSchedule; method list taken from the install's own doc-html/runtime-api.json rather than guessed."
    ],
    "controls": [
        "'no copy_settings called at all' is what makes the first case mean anything: without a copy the target stays empty AND the blueprint lists only the source in `locomotives` ([25], not [25,26]). So the merged entry in the other cases records a real copy, not create_blueprint grouping trains together.",
        "Each case puts its two locomotives on disconnected rail segments and asserts `sourceAndTargetAreTheSameTrain` is false. Two locomotives on one segment are one train and share a schedule automatically, which would look exactly like a successful copy."
    ],
